      "updated_at": "2018-05-03T15:48:57Z",
      "body": "@mkendall07  I dove through AMP a bit to understand the `amp-consent` tag, and how it relates to `amp-ad` and `amp-iframe`. The former is used for PBS auctions, and the latter for PBS cookie syncs.\r\n\r\nHere are some highlights:\r\n\r\n1. [This comment](https://github.com/ampproject/amphtml/issues/13716#issuecomment-386115655) says that the `amp-consent` tag only exposes [an enum value](https://github.com/ampproject/amphtml/blob/master/extensions/amp-consent/customizing-extension-behaviors-on-consent.md#advanced-blocking-behaviors) to other elements. There won't be enough info there for us to make bidder-specific decisions. \r\n\r\n2. Consent status [is available to `amp-ad` extensions](https://github.com/ampproject/amphtml/issues/14921), but `amp-iframe` ones are a [work in progress](https://github.com/ampproject/amphtml/issues/14877)... so we couldn't use it in our `amp-iframe` yet even if we wanted to.\r\n\r\n3. We _may_ be able to pass this flag into the actual `auction` call, depending on the ad network being used. `amp-ad` implementations can define [their own macros](https://github.com/ampproject/amphtml/blob/master/extensions/amp-a4a/rtc-documentation.md#fast-fetch-implementation-defined-macros), which we could then encode into [our vendor URL](https://github.com/ampproject/amphtml/blob/master/extensions/amp-a4a/0.1/callout-vendors.js#L45). I doubt any ad networks are doing this yet, so we would have to submit PRs to the networks we care about, or convince them. As an example, the doubleclick code is [here](https://github.com/ampproject/amphtml/blob/master/extensions/amp-ad-network-doubleclick-impl/0.1/amp-ad-network-doubleclick-impl.js#L716)",
      "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "pin": null,
